# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: alfe-clautolisp-backend-semantic-parity — keep direct and subprocess clautolisp semantics identical
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-alfe-clautolisp-backend-semantic-parity, alfe-parity-interactive
#+DATE: 2026-10-06

* Context

=alfe --clautolisp= has two execution variants:

- the default =--backend direct= embeds the clautolisp runtime in alfe;
- =--backend subprocess= launches the standalone =clautolisp= program.

These are two transports for the same engine, not two user-visible products.
Selecting the subprocess variant for isolation must not change the meaning or
availability of an option shared by alfe and clautolisp.

The divergence was observed in SCHME job 527154 with alfe 2.2.150:

#+begin_example
alfe: alfe CLAUTOLISP backend error (phase BOOTSTRAP):
Failed to initialise the clautolisp runtime:
no reader codec registered for format :DWG [RUNTIME-INIT-FAILED]
#+end_example

The invocation already contained =--clautolisp --host cador --dwg
Schema_Test.dwg=.  The temporary downstream workaround is to add =--backend
subprocess= to the SCHME cador suites.

* Problem

The two variants do not contain the same runtime capabilities.

=clautolisp/clautolisp-tool= depends on =clautolisp/drawing-dwg=, so the
standalone program registers the =:DWG= reader/writer codec.  The alfe tool
depends on =autolisp-front-end=, whose in-process clautolisp graph does not
load that optional system.  Consequently the same =--dwg= option is parsed
and forwarded to =host-open-startup-drawing= in direct mode, but fails before
the action plan runs because no DWG codec is registered.

This is a semantic difference, not merely an implementation detail:

#+begin_example
alfe --clautolisp --host cador --dwg file.dwg ...
alfe --clautolisp --backend subprocess --host cador --dwg file.dwg ...
#+end_example

The first and second commands are expected to expose the same clautolisp
features and observable results.  A caller must not need to know which ASDF
systems happen to have been baked into the alfe image.

The risk is broader than =--dwg=.  Shared CLI parsing, duplicated direct-mode
initialisation and selective subprocess forwarding can let any present or
future option work in only one variant, or use different defaults, environment
variables, diagnostics, side effects or exit statuses.

* Proposal

1. Define one explicit capability and option contract for alfe's clautolisp
   backend.  Both variants must consume the same resolved configuration.
2. Make the direct alfe image load every clautolisp subsystem required to
   implement that contract.  In particular, load =clautolisp/drawing-dwg= so
   =--dwg= registers the same codec as the standalone program while preserving
   lazy loading of the native library.
3. Derive subprocess arguments and direct-mode initialisation from the same
   option descriptors or resolved configuration, instead of maintaining two
   independent lists.
4. Audit every option accepted for =--clautolisp=, including host, dialect,
   drawing, source/file/terminal encodings, init policy, action ordering,
   debugger/error policy, dribble, DCL/UI selection, optimisation and future
   shared options.  Remove documented direct/subprocess exceptions by
   implementing equivalent behaviour rather than silently ignoring an option.
5. When exact parity is structurally impossible, reject the option explicitly
   and identically before starting either engine.  Do not accept it and then
   silently change its meaning according to the backend variant.

* Acceptance criteria

- Both commands above open the same DWG and run the same action plan with the
  same observable AutoLISP state, stdout, stderr and exit status.
- =alfe --clautolisp --dwg FILE= works in the default direct variant whenever
  the installed native DWG libraries support the subprocess variant.
- A table-driven regression suite runs every option common to the clautolisp
  backend once with =--backend direct= and once with =--backend subprocess=.
- The parity suite compares resolved configuration, action ordering, output,
  diagnostics, exit status and relevant file/drawing side effects.
- Adding a new shared clautolisp option requires adding it once to the common
  option contract; CI fails if either variant does not consume it.
- =alfe --help=, the alfe user manual, man page and specifications state the
  semantic-parity guarantee and no longer document backend-variant differences
  for shared options.
- The SCHME workaround (=--backend subprocess= on the cador suites) can be
  removed after a release containing the fix is consumed.

* Progress (2026-10-06, alfe 2.2.158 / clautolisp 2.2.212)

Branch =fix-alfe-clautolisp-backend-semantic-parity=. The reported failure
is fixed and most of the contract is in place; the ticket stays open for
the divergences listed under /Remaining/.

** Done

- *Capabilities.* =autolisp-front-end/backend-clautolisp= now depends on
  =clautolisp/drawing-dwg= and =clautolisp/autolisp-compiler=, as
  =clautolisp/clautolisp-tool= does. =alfe --clautolisp --host cador --dwg
  FILE.dwg= opens the drawing in the default direct variant; the native
  LibreDWG shim stays lazily loaded (=ensure-shim-loaded= at the first
  conversion), so an alfe without it still starts. Verified with the built
  =alfe-sbcl= on =autolisp-front-end/source/empty.dwg=: =DWGNAME= printed
  identically, exit 0, by =--backend direct= and =--backend subprocess=.
- *One resolved configuration.* The =*AUTOLISP-...*= bindings are computed
  once (=direct-transmit-bindings=). Direct installs them; subprocess
  writes them to a file and passes the new clautolisp option
  =--front-end-bindings FILE= (format in
  =clautolisp/autolisp-cli/source/engine.lisp=), which the child installs
  instead of its own. Before: =*AUTOLISP-FRONTEND*= CLAUTOLISP,
  =*AUTOLISP-QUIET*= T, =*AUTOLISP-VERSION*= clautolisp's, =PROGRAM=
  "clautolisp" in the child. Now only =*AUTOLISP-PROCESS*= differs (DIRECT
  / SUBPROCESS), by design.
- *Same run.* The direct variant now links the startup drawing's LISP
  namespace (=link-runtime-session-to-host=, multi-document slice 1),
  applies a pending document switch before each action, binds
  =*AUTOLISP-EXPRESSION*= during =-x=, and runs =--main FN= as =-x
  "(FN)"= -- all as the clautolisp program does.
- *Same outcome.* The program's reporters moved to =autolisp-cli=
  (=report-autolisp-runtime-error=, =report-autolisp-termination=,
  =report-engine-error=, =engine-drawing-error-message=) and both variants
  use them. =eval-result= gained =exit-code=: the engine's status passes
  through in both variants (=(exit N)=, =(quit N)=, =autolisp-set-status=
  -> N; runtime error 1; unopenable =-l= file 2; unreadable =--dwg= 1 with
  =--dwg: cannot open the drawing ...=). Before, direct exited 0 on =(exit
  3)= and subprocess squashed every status to 1. NOTE for pjb: an
  unopenable =-l= file is now 2 in both variants (the clautolisp program's
  documented code); it was 1 in-process. The conformance scenario
  =actions-load-missing-file= was updated. If alfe's own table (2 = usage
  error) must win, the child has to tell a file error from a user =(exit
  2)=, which needs a channel it does not have today.
- *Contract.* =alfe.backend.clautolisp:*clautolisp-option-contract*=
  (=source/clautolisp-option-contract.lisp=) classifies every option alfe
  accepts; =clautolisp-option-contract-classifies-every-accepted-option=
  fails when one is missing or stale.
- *Parity suite.* =clautolisp-backend-variants-are-semantically-identical=
  runs 18 rows (host cador/nihil, --dialect, --lax, the bindings, --quiet
  --timeout --mode --no-color, -l, -x, --main, runtime error, exit and
  recorded status, missing -l file, -Esource, -Efile-write with the written
  octets, --dwg on a DXF, a missing drawing, and empty.dwg) through
  =alfe.cli:run= under both variants, comparing stdout, stderr, exit
  status and written files. -Eterminal is compared on the resolved
  configuration (it needs a real terminal fd).
- *Docs.* alfe user manual (/One engine, two transports/), specification
  (/Semantic parity of the two modes/), alfe(1), =alfe --help=,
  clautolisp manual and clautolisp(1) for =--front-end-bindings=.

** Option audit

| Option(s)                                         | Disposition | Direct                                          | Subprocess                                  |
|---------------------------------------------------+-------------+-------------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------|
| --help --version --list-* --compile-plugin        | front-end   | answered by alfe                                | answered by alfe                            |
| --dry-run --print-command                         | front-end   | alfe; --print-command is a usage error          | same                                        |
| --clautolisp --autocad --bricscad --cad --backend | front-end   | backend / variant selection                     | same                                        |
| --workdir --keep-workdir --write-workdir-path     | front-end   | alfe workdir; *AUTOLISP-WORKDIR* via bindings   | same                                        |
| --plugin --plugin-path --no-plugins               | front-end   | alfe plug-ins                                   | same (but see per-action hooks below)       |
| --on-error                                        | front-end   | alfe's own conditions                           | same                                        |
| -q -v -d                                          | front-end   | alfe log level; *AUTOLISP-QUIET* ... via bindings | child always --quiet; same bindings       |
| --no-init                                         | front-end   | alfe init files become -l actions               | same; child always --no-init                |
| --dialect --strict --lax                          | engine      | resolve-clautolisp-dialect                      | --dialect NAME                              |
| --host                                            | engine      | resolve-clautolisp-host                         | --host NAME (cadtui: subprocess only)       |
| --dwg                                             | engine      | host-open-startup-drawing (codec now loaded)    | --dwg FILE                                  |
| -l -x --main --quit                               | engine      | in-process, same bindings and switches          | -l / -x / -x "(FN)" / implicit              |
| --no-color                                        | engine      | *color-output*                                  | $NO_COLOR                                   |
| -E --source-encoding                              | engine      | session default source encoding                 | -Esource                                    |
| --file[-read/-write]-encoding                     | engine      | *AUTOLISP-FILE-READ/WRITE-ENCODING*             | -Efile-read / -Efile-write                  |
| --log-encoding                                    | engine      | *AUTOLISP-LOG-ENCODING*                         | -Elog                                       |
| --terminal[-input/-output]-encoding, --console-*  | engine      | alfe's own streams (console folded in)          | -Eterminal-in/-out + capture decoding       |
| --mode --bootstrap-phase --timeout --cadstdio-*   | no-effect   | not acted on                                    | not acted on                                |
| -i (and a bare alfe)                              | program     | default: the child; explicit: usage error (2)   | the clautolisp program (child)              |
| --dribble --dribble-interactors                   | program     | the child records its REPL (batch: nothing)     | same                                        |
| --dcl                                             | program     | tui/auto: line renderer; ncurses/gui: the child | forwarded as --dcl; same                    |

(See the 2026-10-06 second progress section: the last three rows were
DIVERGENT until alfe 2.2.159.)

** Remaining (as of alfe 2.2.158; items 1-3 and 5 done since, see below)

1. =-i=: the direct REPL is alfe's own minimal loop; the subprocess is the
   clautolisp REPL (interactors, comma commands, aldo debugger on error by
   the interactive =--on-error= default). Either embed the clautolisp REPL
   (needs the tool's repl-loop / interactor / debugger systems in alfe) or
   route =-i= to the subprocess as =--host cadtui= is.
2. =--dribble=: only the subprocess records. Same two options as =-i=
   (the recorder is the clautolisp program's).
3. =--dcl= and DCL itself: =autolisp-dcl= and the renderer selection
   (=select-and-install-dcl-renderer=) live in the clautolisp program; the
   in-process engine has no DCL. alfe also does not forward =--dcl=.
4. Plug-ins with =:pre-action= / =:post-action= hooks:
   =%eval-plan-with-hooks= calls EVAL-PLAN once per action, and the
   subprocess variant spawns one child per call, so state does not carry
   from one action to the next there (it does in-process).
5. The subprocess argv is still assembled by hand (=build-subprocess-argv=);
   the contract table is checked for completeness, not used to derive the
   argv.
6. Verify on the native Windows / macOS lanes, with an INSTALLED alfe (the
   DWG shim is searched from alfe's own prefix:
   =<PREFIX>/lib/clautolisp/<os>/<arch>/=), and on CCL.
7. Decide the exit status of an unopenable =-l= file (see the NOTE above).
8. The SCHME workaround (=--backend subprocess= on the cador suites) can be
   dropped once a release containing alfe 2.2.158 is consumed.


* Progress (2026-10-06, second part: alfe 2.2.159, branch alfe-parity-interactive)

** Approach chosen for -i / --dribble / --dcl: the program's runs are the program's

Two ways were open: embed the clautolisp program's REPL, recorder and DCL
renderer selection in alfe's image, or run those sessions in the clautolisp
program, as =--host cadtui= already was.

Embedding was examined and rejected. The REPL is not a library function of
the program: =run-with-input= / =repl-loop= / =main= in
=clautolisp/tools/clautolisp/source/main.lisp= weave together the dribble
tee streams (installed before the debug session captures its streams), the
aldo companion thread, the interrupt handler, the debugger UI selection
(aldo.conf, ncurses, the aldb TCP / stdio transports), process-global hooks
(=*dribble-hook*=, the REPL hooks) and =uiop:quit= on =,quit=. Pulling that
into alfe means loading =clautolisp/clautolisp-tool= (cadtui, the debug UIs,
usocket, bordeaux-threads) into alfe's image and refactoring the program's
=main= into a callable that does not quit -- a second entry point into the
same machinery, with its global state living inside alfe's process (and
alfe's in-process test image). That is the duplication the ticket is about,
moved rather than removed.

Routing instead: =alfe.cli:clautolisp-program-requirement= names what makes a
run the program's -- an interactive session (=-i=, or no -l / -x / --main /
FILE), =--dribble=, =--dcl ncurses= / =gui=, =--host cadtui= -- and
=%clautolisp-variant= picks the subprocess variant for it unless =--backend
direct= was explicit, which is a usage error (exit 2) naming the reason, as
cadtui was. The same program then runs with the same argv whichever way the
user spelled it: identical by construction, no second REPL. alfe's own
minimal =alfe>= loop (=direct-interactive=, =read-balanced-source=) and the
"--dribble records nothing" warning are deleted; an =:interactive= action
handed to a direct session by a caller bypassing alfe.cli is a
=backend-eval-error= (=:interactive-needs-subprocess=).

=--dcl tui= / =auto= stay in-process: in a batch run the child's stdout is a
captured pipe, so its =auto= picks the line renderer, which is the renderer
the in-process engine has (=autolisp-dcl= is already in alfe's image through
=autolisp-builtins-core=; the "no DCL in-process" of the audit was wrong).
=--dcl= is now forwarded to the child when not =auto=, and =ncurses= / =gui=
runs get alfe's terminal like a REPL.

Standard streams: the child now reads alfe's standard input in every run
(batch included: a =GETSTRING= or a line DCL dialog reads the same input as
in-process, instead of an empty one). A stream that is not a process stream
(a test's or an embedder's string stream) is handed to uiop, which copies
it; the process's own descriptors are inherited (=%child-stream=; SBCL
=fd-stream=, CCL =stream-device=).

** Item 5 done: the argv is derived from the contract

Each =:engine= / =:program= entry of =*clautolisp-option-contract*= now says
with =:forward= how the child receives it: argv-fragment functions of the
session (=%forward-dialect=, =%forward-host=, =%forward-dwg=,
=%forward-source-encoding=, =%situation-cli-flags=, =%dribble-cli-flags=,
=%forward-dcl=), =:plan= (an action) or =:environment= ($NO_COLOR).
=build-subprocess-argv= is the fixed prefix (=--quiet --no-init=), every
distinct forwarder of the table in table order, the front-end bindings, then
the actions. Tests: every engine/program entry has a usable =:forward= and no
other entry has one; a fully configured session's argv carries each option
once; the default argv is exactly =--quiet --no-init --dialect strict --host
cador=.

** Tests

- Contract: new disposition =:program= (=--interactive=, =--dribble=,
  =--dribble-interactors=, =--dcl=); =:divergent= is empty.
- Parity table, now 27 rows: =-i= with stdin, a bare REPL, actions then REPL
  (state carries), a REPL runtime error, dribble of a REPL (file compared
  octet for octet and checked for the form and its output), dribble of a
  batch run (no file in either), DCL with =--dcl tui=, DCL by default, and a
  dialog answered on standard input (=(RESULT 1)= both ways). Program rows
  compare the default with =--backend subprocess=.
- =--backend direct= with =-i=, a bare run, =--dribble=, =--dcl ncurses=:
  exit 2, nothing on stdout, the reason on stderr.
- Routing unit tests (=cli-clautolisp-program-runs-go-to-the-clautolisp-
  executable=); conformance scenarios =program-runs-dry-run=,
  =program-runs-not-direct=, =repl-not-direct= (no engine needed, so they
  hold in a CI lane without clautolisp-sbcl; the suite was also run with the
  binary hidden: 0 failures). =dcl-and-dribble-accepted= keeps =--dcl tui=
  and =--dribble-interactors= only (still in-process).

Found on the way: =clautolisp-load-dialog-first-id-is-zero.issue= (the
first =load_dialog= returns 0, not a positive handle).

** Still remaining

4. Plug-ins with =:pre-action= / =:post-action= hooks under the subprocess
   variant: one child per action, so AutoLISP state does not carry from one
   action to the next (it does in-process). Now also reached WITHOUT
   =--backend subprocess= when such a plug-in is active and the run is a
   REPL / =--dribble= one: the REPL child starts without what the earlier
   actions (init files included) defined. A fix needs a persistent child
   (actions sent to one engine over a framed channel, outputs and status
   delimited per action) -- the "v2" design of start-subprocess-engine --
   because the REPL's echo of values makes "-i and feed the forms" differ
   from batch output. Documented in the spec (Semantic parity, point 8).
6. Native Windows / macOS lanes, an INSTALLED alfe, and CCL: unchanged from
   above. CCL: =make -C autolisp-front-end test-ccl= runs every new row
   green (so the CCL branch of =%process-stream-p= works), but ONE row
   that predates this branch fails there: "missing load file". A CCL alfe
   in-process reports the =file-error= in CCL's words (=clautolisp: No such
   file or directory : "/nonexistent/..."=), the SBCL child in SBCL's
   (=clautolisp: The file #P"/nonexistent/..." does not exist: ...=).
   =report-engine-error= (autolisp-cli) prints the host Lisp's condition
   text; it should render a =file-error= itself (pathname + OS reason) so the
   message does not depend on which Lisp built which program.
7. pjb: the exit status of an unopenable =-l= file (see the NOTE above).
8. The SCHME workaround can go once a release with alfe 2.2.158+ is consumed.
9. =(clal-dribble ...)= CALLED from a batch script, without =--dribble=: a
   no-op in-process (the recorder is the program's), recording in the
   subprocess variant. Cannot be routed statically; it would need the
   recorder in a library both load, or every run in the child.
